@* External Geometry Interface. This program reads a geometry 
specification from an external file, and transforms it into the form
required by Degas 2. Namely, the hiearchy of geometry-related objects in
Degas 2 is, from the lowest level to the highest level is:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item surface,
  \item cell,
  \item polygon,
  \item zone.
\end{enumerate}
Individual cells are composed of surfaces, as discussed in the documentation
for the internal geometry (e.g., see |geometry.web|). The next level up
is a polygon. The code automatically breaks up (non-convex) polygons into
cells. Finally, a zone may consist of one or more polygons; properties
are constant across a zone. For example, a single zone might be used to
represent the vacuum region around the plasma which is comprised of several
(possibly disconnected) polygons. Or, a plasma flux surface on which density
and temperature are constant might be a single zone. Note that polygons
are used only within external interfaces such as this code. Cells and surfaces
are essentially used only by the code itself. Zones are used primarily for the
specification of input and output data.

The interfaces between the plasma (or vacuum)  and a solid surface (or exit)
are represented by sectors, which are established for diagnostic purposes.
In general, a sector is defined by a surface and a zone. This code makes 
an attempt to identify and label these sectors automatically. 

The command line for the execution of this code must specify a file name.
The syntax for each line in that file is:

{\bf keyword} {\it arguments } 

In the following, keywords will be bold-faced, arguments will be italicized.

  \begin{enumerate}
    \item {\bf degasfile} {\it file\_name} provides the name of the 
Degas input file.
    \item  {\bf minx} {\it x\_value}  and  {\bf minz} {\it z\_value} tell the
code to exclude grid indices for which the gridx, gridz values lie 
below these {\it x} and {\it z}.
    \item {\bf mindensity} {\it density\_value} is used to exclude 
grid indices which correspond to cells having a plasma density less than the 
given value.
    \item {\bf grid} {\it j1 j2 j3 j4 sense} is used to transform the gridx
and gridz arrays into Degas 2 zones.
    \begin{itemize}
      \item The vertical boundary (or horizontal zone, i.e., the
first index of gridx and gridz) 
and horizontal boundary (or vertical zone, the second index) limits are 
{\it j1}, {\it j2} and  {\it j3}, {\it j4}, repsectively.
      \item For each pair of integer coordinates in this implied list,
a separate Degas 2 zone of type |plasma| will be generated. Note that this
includes points at {\it j2 + 1} and {\it j4 + 1} (hence, these are more
properly referred to as the original Degas ``zone indices'').
      \item Zones can be excluded from this list by using {\bf minx},
{\bf minz}, or {\bf mindensity} entries in this input file. The code will
also ignore zones in which the Degas arrays kzone1 or kzone2 are $\leq 0$.
      \item {\it sense} is optional. Allowed values are {\it sense} = 1 
(default) and 2. A value of 1 indicates that a clockwise trip around
a single cell requires incrementing the {\em first} index of gridx and gridz
initially, then incrementing the second index to get the third point, etc.
Likewise, a value of 2 implies that the {\em second} index must be
incremented first to make the clockwise trip.
    \end{itemize}
    \item {\bf new\_zone} {\it zone\_type}  starts a new zone; {\it zone\_type}
is a string that indicates the type of  the zone. It can be 
      \begin{enumerate}
        \item {\it plasma},
        \item {\it vacuum}, 
        \item {\it solid}.
      \end{enumerate}
    \item {\bf new\_polygon} starts a new polygon. This line should be 
followed by one or more lines which provide the code with the polygon
coordinates, and then a line containing \ldots
    \item {\bf define\_polygon} which terminates the definition of the current
polygon begun by the previous {\bf new\_polygon} and adds it to the 
current zone. 
    \item {\bf wall} {\it wall\_number start stop direction} takes points
from the Degas xwall and zwall arrays and adds them to the current polygon.
    \begin{itemize}
      \item {\it start} \& {\it stop} can be replaced by ``*'' 
(meaning the whole wall segment),
      \item {\it direction} is optional (default = +1),
    \end{itemize}
Unless overridden by explicit use of the {\bf stratum} keyword, the code will
use the wall number as the stratum number. Also, it will attempt to extract
and use the material symbol contained in the |kwmat| array; this can be
overridden by the use of the {\bf material} keyword. If it fails to interpret
the entries in |kwmat| and a material is not specified, an error will occur.
Note that these overrides must be repeated with each appearance of the
{\bf wall} keyword (i.e., after {\bf wall}, but before {\bf define\_polygon}).
    \item {\bf outer} {\it i j k} adds the $i$th $j$th $k$th points of the 
bounding rectangle of the problem (minimum, maximum $x$ and $z$ determined 
by the code) to the current polygon. The numbering of the corners is clockwise
starting from 0 at $(x_{min}, z_{min})$. There can be any number of arguments,
although typical usage will be with three.
    \item {\bf edge} {\it j1 j2 j3 j4} takes an ``edge'' (i.e., a section
of a horizontal or vertical boundary) from the Degas gridx, gridz arrays
and adds it to the current polygon. The 
arguments indicate the same things as in  grid except that here we must have 
either {\it j1} = {\it j2} or {\it j3} = {\it j4} in order for the edge to
lie on a single boundary.
    \item {\bf symmetry} {\it plane} or {\it cylindrical} specifies the
overall symmetry.  (Better can first.)
    \item {\bf wallfile} {\it filename} specifies a file with two wall
segments to go with uedge files.
    \item {\bf stratum} {\it j} Sets the value of |current_stratum| to {\it j}.
Subsequent polygons ``defined'' will be associated with this stratum until
|current_stratum| is again changed by the appearance of this keyword in the
file.
    \item {\bf material} {\it material\_symbol} assigns the |current_material|
to the material represented by {\it material\_symbol} which must be one of the
legal values of |materials_sy| as read in by the |materials_setup| routine.
Subsequent polygons ``defined'' will be made of this material until
|current_material| is again changed by the appearance of this keyword in the
file.  
\end{enumerate}

A typical file might have lines in the following order:
\begin{verbatim}

degasfile ...

minx ...
minz ...
mindensity ...
grid

new_zone ...
  new_polygon
    material ...
    stratum ...
    wall ...
    outer ...
    edge ...
  define_polygon

  new_polygon
    .
    .
    .

new_zone
   .
   .
   .

end
\end{verbatim}

